{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000104",
  "title": "Conway's Game of Life",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# Conway's Game of Life\n\n**Conway's Game of Life** is a zero-player cellular automaton devised by mathematician John Conway in 1970. It takes place on a 2D grid of cells, each either **alive** or **dead**.\n\n## Rules (B3/S23)\n\nAt each generation, every cell's fate is determined by its eight neighbors:\n\n- **Birth**: A dead cell with exactly **3** live neighbors becomes alive.\n- **Survival**: A live cell with **2 or 3** live neighbors stays alive.\n- **Death**: All other live cells die (loneliness or overcrowding).\n\nThese simple rules give rise to astonishing complexity \u2014 gliders, oscillators, and even Turing-complete logic circuits.\n\n## This Notebook\n\nWe seed a 64\u00d764 toroidal grid with an **R-pentomino** (a chaotic methuselah pattern) and a **glider**, then run 100 generations. The first cell renders the final state; the second shows a filmstrip of the evolution.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Simulation & Final State",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\nconst W: usize = 64;\nconst H: usize = 64;\nconst PX: u32 = 8;\n\n// Initialize grid\nlet mut grid = vec![vec![false; W]; H];\n\n// Seed: R-pentomino at center (a methuselah that takes 1103 gens to stabilize)\nlet (cx, cy) = (W / 2, H / 2);\ngrid[cy - 1][cx] = true;\ngrid[cy - 1][cx + 1] = true;\ngrid[cy][cx - 1] = true;\ngrid[cy][cx] = true;\ngrid[cy + 1][cx] = true;\n\n// Seed: Glider in top-left corner\ngrid[1][2] = true;\ngrid[2][3] = true;\ngrid[3][1] = true;\ngrid[3][2] = true;\ngrid[3][3] = true;\n\n// Step function: B3/S23 with toroidal wrapping\nfn step(grid: &[Vec<bool>]) -> Vec<Vec<bool>> {\n    let (h, w) = (grid.len(), grid[0].len());\n    let mut next = vec![vec![false; w]; h];\n    for y in 0..h {\n        for x in 0..w {\n            let mut n = 0u8;\n            for dy in [h - 1, 0, 1] {\n                for dx in [w - 1, 0, 1] {\n                    if dy == 0 && dx == 0 { continue; }\n                    if grid[(y + dy) % h][(x + dx) % w] { n += 1; }\n                }\n            }\n            next[y][x] = n == 3 || (n == 2 && grid[y][x]);\n        }\n    }\n    next\n}\n\n// Run 100 generations, capturing snapshots at key points\nlet snapshot_gens = [0usize, 25, 50, 75, 100];\nlet mut snapshots: Vec<Vec<u8>> = Vec::new();\n\n// Capture generation 0\nsnapshots.push(\n    grid.iter().flat_map(|r| r.iter().map(|&c| u8::from(c))).collect()\n);\n\nfor gen in 1..=100 {\n    grid = step(&grid);\n    if snapshot_gens.contains(&gen) {\n        snapshots.push(\n            grid.iter().flat_map(|r| r.iter().map(|&c| u8::from(c))).collect()\n        );\n    }\n}\n\n// Render final state (generation 100) as SVG\nlet (img_w, img_h) = (W as u32 * PX, H as u32 * PX);\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (img_w, img_h))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(20, 20, 30)).unwrap();\n\n    let live = RGBColor(0, 210, 130);\n    for y in 0..H {\n        let mut x = 0;\n        while x < W {\n            if grid[y][x] {\n                let start = x;\n                while x < W && grid[y][x] { x += 1; }\n                root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n                    [((start as u32 * PX) as i32, (y as u32 * PX) as i32),\n                     ((x as u32 * PX) as i32 - 1, ((y as u32 + 1) * PX) as i32 - 1)],\n                    live.filled(),\n                )).unwrap();\n            } else {\n                x += 1;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\n(snapshots, Svg(svg_buf))\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_1\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Evolution Filmstrip",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\nconst W: usize = 64;\nconst H: usize = 64;\nconst PX: u32 = 4;\n\nlet labels = [\"Gen 0\", \"Gen 25\", \"Gen 50\", \"Gen 75\", \"Gen 100\"];\nlet (img_w, img_h) = (W as u32 * PX, H as u32 * PX);\n\nlet mut html = String::from(\n    \"<div style='display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center'>\"\n);\n\nfor (flat, label) in cell1.iter().zip(labels.iter()) {\n    let mut svg = String::new();\n    {\n        let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg, (img_w, img_h))\n            .into_drawing_area();\n        root.fill(&RGBColor(20, 20, 30)).unwrap();\n\n        let live = RGBColor(0, 210, 130);\n        for y in 0..H {\n            let mut x = 0;\n            while x < W {\n                if flat[y * W + x] == 1 {\n                    let start = x;\n                    while x < W && flat[y * W + x] == 1 { x += 1; }\n                    root.draw(&Rectangle::new(\n                        [((start as u32 * PX) as i32, (y as u32 * PX) as i32),\n                         ((x as u32 * PX) as i32 - 1, ((y as u32 + 1) * PX) as i32 - 1)],\n                        live.filled(),\n                    )).unwrap();\n                } else {\n                    x += 1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        root.present().unwrap();\n    }\n\n    html.push_str(&format!(\n        \"<div style='text-align:center'><div style='color:#aaa; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:4px'>{label}</div>{svg}</div>\"\n    ));\n}\n\nhtml.push_str(\"</div>\");\nHtml(html)\n",
      "cargo_toml": "[package]\nname = \"cell_2\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[dependencies]\nironpad-cell = \"0.1\"\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Emergent Behavior",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Emergent Behavior\n\nDespite its three simple rules, the Game of Life produces rich emergent phenomena:\n\n- **Gliders** travel diagonally across the grid indefinitely.\n- **Oscillators** (blinkers, pulsars) cycle between fixed states.\n- **Methuselahs** like the R-pentomino take hundreds of generations to stabilize, producing a burst of activity from a tiny seed.\n- **Glider guns** produce an infinite stream of gliders.\n\n### Try Modifying\n\n- Seed the grid with a **Gosper glider gun** or a random fill.\n- Increase the grid size or number of generations.\n- Experiment with different boundary conditions (fixed edges vs. toroidal wrapping).\n- Try alternative rule sets: **HighLife** (B36/S23) adds a self-replicating pattern.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ]
}
